      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for addressing our previous comments. There are still some issues to be resolved. In general, I observed the following systematic issue with the change set:\n\n1. Lack of documentation\n\nThere has been almost no JavaDoc throughout this change set and it is hard for a reader to understand the flow of code without comments and JavaDoc. Could you please add more documentation and comments to this patch? \n\n2. Unformatted use of whitespaces\n\nLots of code has random gaps that break the code flow, for example:\n\nin android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/bass_client/BassClientStateMachine.java\n```\n                    log(\n                            \"onSyncEstablished\"\n                                    + \"syncHandle\"\n                                    + syncHandle\n                                    + \"device\"\n                                    + device\n                                    + \"advertisingSid\"\n                                    + advertisingSid\n                                    + \"skip\"\n                                    + skip\n                                    + \"timeout\"\n                                    + timeout\n                                    + \"status\"\n                                    + status);\n```\n\nThe gaps in the above code is not necessary and should be reformatted to make the code more readable. \n\nAlso some code has random blank lines that should be removed.\n\n3. C-style use of Java: Separated variable declaration and assignment\n\nMany parts of the code have separate variable declaration and assignment. In Java and modern C++, they should stay close to each other so that their scope is minimized. For example:\n\nIn `convertSourceInfoToAddSourceByteArray` in android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/bass_client/BassClientStateMachine.java\n\n```\n    private byte[] convertSourceInfoToAddSourceByteArray(BluetoothLeBroadcastSourceInfo srcInfo) {\n        byte[] res;\n        /*fixed length for add source op*/\n        int addSourceFixedLength \u003d 16;\n        .................................\n        .................................\n            }\n            totalMetadataLength \u003d totalMetadataLength + metaDataLength[i];\n        }\n        res \u003d new byte[addSourceFixedLength + numSubGroups * 5 + totalMetadataLength];\n        srcInfo.setSourceDevice(broadcastSource);\n```\nres is not used until much further down the code\n\nMoreover\n\n```\n        boolean setSuccessfully;\n        setSuccessfully \u003d\n```\nThis one has declaration one line away from assignment and can be simplified into one line. \n\n4. Constants and Magic Numbers\n\nLots of magic numbers were used without a constant definition. Please declare them into named constants so that a reader knows the meaning of the constant. Such as\n\n```\n    private void notifyLocalBroadcastSourceFound(\n            ArrayList\u003cIBluetoothLeBroadcastAssistantCallback\u003e cbs) {\n.....\n        ScanResult scanRes \u003d new ScanResult(localDev, 1, 1, 1, 2, 0, 0, 0, record, 0);\n```\n\nThe numbers `1, 1, 1, 2,` are not readable\n\nand\n\n```\n\n    private int convertConfigurationSRToCapabilitySR(byte sampleRate) {\n        int ret \u003d BluetoothCodecConfig.SAMPLE_RATE_NONE;\n        switch (sampleRate) {\n            case 1:\n                ret \u003d BluetoothCodecConfig.SAMPLE_RATE_NONE;\n                break;\n            case 2:\n                ret \u003d BluetoothCodecConfig.SAMPLE_RATE_NONE;\n                break;\n            case 3:\n                ret \u003d BluetoothCodecConfig.SAMPLE_RATE_NONE;\n```\n\nThe meaning of 1, 2 and 3 is not clear\n\nMoreover there are constants everywhere in the codebase. Instead, it would be better to organize constants in groups into a single file such as BassConstants.java\n\n5. Too much nesting\n\nFor example, in `searchforBroadcastSources`, instead of \n```\n                        if (scanRecord !\u003d null) {\n                                ...\n                                if (listOfUuids !\u003d null) {\n                                    ...\n                                    if (isBroadcastSource) {\n                                        ...\n                                        if (cbs !\u003d null) {\n                                            for (...) {\n                                                try {\n                                                    ...\n                                                } catch (RemoteException e) {\n                                                    Log.e(TAG,\n                                                }\n                                            }\n                                        } else {\n```\n\nPlease adopt check error early and return first pattern to reduce the nesting\n\n```\nif (scanRecord \u003d\u003d null) {\n  log(ERROR)\n  return;\n}\n```\n\nThis will make the code much easier to read. \n\n6. Architecture is ready for unit testing\n\nThe entire BASS Client implementation is done in 4 classes.\nBaseData.java: 662 lines\nBassClientStateMachine.java: 1996 lines\nBassUtils.java: 348 lines\nBCService.java: 1554 lines\n\nIt is not very clear what are the functions of each class and how we can test each of these classes without exposing all its private variables. Could we think about how we can break these classes into smaller layers (e.g. SystemInterface, BassScanManager, BassGattClient, etc) so that unit tests can be written for the implementation and dependency classes can be mocked. \n\nThanks",
